window.OPOSSUM_FOOT_GUIDE_HTML = "<p>A trap log built by a trapper, for trappers. If you can run a trap line, you can run this app. This guide walks through everything one tap at a time.</p>\n<h3>The idea in thirty seconds</h3>\n<p>You already know where your sets are. What you don't know — not really — is what's <em>producing</em>. Which lure, which bait, which trap, which location is actually putting fur up? Write it down every time in this app, and at the end of the season it tells you. No more guessing in February.</p>\n<h3>Putting it on your phone</h3>\n<p><strong>iPhone:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Open the beta link in Safari (not Messenger, not Facebook — Safari).</li><li>Tap the Share button (the square with the arrow pointing up).</li><li>Scroll down and tap \"Add to Home Screen.\"</li><li>Tap \"Add.\" You'll see the Opossum Foot icon on your home screen now. Open</li></ol>\n<p>the app from that icon from here on out.</p>\n<p><strong>Android:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Open the beta link in Chrome.</li><li>Tap the three-dot menu in the corner.</li><li>Tap \"Add to Home screen\" (or \"Install app\").</li><li>Open the app from the new icon.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>If a link comes to you in Messenger:</strong> Messenger can't add things to your home screen. Long-press the link, tap Copy, open Safari (or Chrome) yourself, paste it in the address bar, and follow the steps above.</p>\n<h3>The first time you open it</h3>\n<p>The app asks you to pick your state. Pick the state you trap in — the map zooms right to it, and your season reminders come from that state's rules. You can change it later in Settings.</p>\n<h3>The tabs along the bottom</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Map</strong> — Your sets as pins on the map. Green pin: active set. Amber:</li></ul>\n<p>sprung. Gray: pulled. Tap any pin to see that set.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Lines</strong> — Your trap lines. More on this below.</li><li><strong>History</strong> — Every catch and event you've logged, with filters.</li><li><strong>Totals</strong> — Your season numbers. The money tab.</li><li><strong>Seasons</strong> — Season dates and reminders for your state, species by species.</li><li><strong>Licenses</strong> — Photos of your trapping licenses, kept on the phone.</li><li><strong>Weather</strong> — Current conditions where you're standing.</li><li><strong>Settings</strong> — Your state, which set fields you want shown, backups, and</li></ul>\n<p>the About screen.</p>\n<h3>Trap lines</h3>\n<p>A \"line\" is a group of sets you run together — say, your home line and the line on your buddy's farm. Each line keeps its own sets, logs, seasons, licenses, history, and totals, all separate.</p>\n<ul><li>Only one line is active at a time. The brass-colored bar near the top of</li></ul>\n<p>the screen always shows which line you're on — tap it to switch.</p>\n<ul><li>To start a new line: go to the Lines tab and tap \"+ Add a trap line.\" Give</li></ul>\n<p>it a name and pick its state.</p>\n<ul><li>When you start out, everything lives on Line 1. That's fine.</li></ul>\n<h3>Adding a set — step by step</h3>\n<ol><li>Go to the <strong>Map</strong> tab.</li><li>Tap the <strong>+</strong> button (or \"Add set at my location\" to drop the pin where</li></ol>\n<p>you're standing). A form opens.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Name your set.</strong> This one's required — \"Creek bend,\" \"Fence corner,\"</li></ol>\n<p>whatever you'll recognize in the dark.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Trap type:</strong> Foothold, Bodygrip / Conibear, Snare, Cage live trap,</li></ol>\n<p>Colony trap, Dog-proof, or Other.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Trap details</strong> change to match the trap you picked:</li></ol>\n<p>- Foothold: pick spring type first (Coil-spring or Longspring), then the size (#1, #1.75, #2, and so on). - Snare: cable diameter, lock type, cable length. - Bodygrip / Colony / Cage: pick the size. - Dog-proof / Other: just the brand and model. - There's a brand/model box on all of them if you want it (\"Bridger #1.75\").</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Number of traps</strong> at this set. It starts at 1 — change it if you run</li></ol>\n<p>doubles.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Bait</strong> and <strong>lure</strong> — what the set is carrying. This is what makes the</li></ol>\n<p>Totals tab worth anything, so fill it in honestly.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Set type</strong> — Dirt hole, Flat set, Pocket set, and the rest, A to Z.</li><li><strong>Status</strong> — Active, Sprung, Pulled, or Other. New sets start Active.</li><li>Add a photo if you want (handy for remembering exactly how you built it).</li><li>Tap <strong>Save set.</strong> Your pin shows up on the map.</li></ol>\n<p>Changed something at a set — fresh bait, pulled the trap? Open the set from the map pin and edit it. The app keeps the history straight.</p>\n<h3>Logging a catch — step by step</h3>\n<ol><li>Tap the set's pin on the map (or find it in History), then tap</li></ol>\n<p><strong>\"＋ Log a catch.\"</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Pick the animal.</strong> The list runs A to Z with a search box at the top —</li></ol>\n<p>type \"coon\" and Raccoon pops right up. If it was a neighbor's dog or a house cat, that's the \"Domestic animal\" row at the very bottom (tap the <strong>?</strong> next to it for what to do).</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Count</strong> — how many.</li><li><strong>Disposition</strong> — what happened to it:</li></ol>\n<p>- <strong>Kept</strong> — it's going on the stretcher. - <strong>Kept alive</strong> — kept, but alive (relocation, sale, whatever your case). - <strong>Released</strong> — let go on the spot. - <strong>Transported</strong> — hauled out and released elsewhere.</p>\n<ol><li>Add a note if you want (\"big boar, tore up the set\"), then save.</li></ol>\n<p>The app snapshots the set's bait, lure, and trap onto the log entry, so even if you change the set later, the record of <em>that catch</em> stays honest.</p>\n<h3>\"Other events\" — the stuff that isn't a catch</h3>\n<p>On the log screen there's a tab for other events. Use it for:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Sprung</strong> — trap fired, nothing in it.</li><li><strong>Non-native animal / Non-game animal</strong> — caught something out of season or</li></ul>\n<p>not a furbearer; the app won't count it as a catch.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Fur</strong> or <strong>Animal part</strong> — found sign worth recording.</li><li><strong>Other</strong> — anything else; describe it in the notes.</li></ul>\n<p>Logging sprung and empty traps matters — it's how the catch-per-night math stays honest.</p>\n<h3>Totals — reading your season</h3>\n<p>Open the <strong>Totals</strong> tab. Everything here is for the current season, highest to lowest.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Catch-rate scorecard (top):</strong> catch per trap-night, ranked by lure or by</li></ul>\n<p>bait (toggle at the top). This answers \"is that $14 lure actually out-catching the cheap stuff?\" with numbers instead of opinions.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>By species:</strong> what you caught, most to least.</li><li><strong>By site:</strong> what each set produced, most to least. Under every site name</li></ul>\n<p>is its setup — bait / lure · trap and size · number of traps · set type. This is the whole app in one screen: *this bait, on this trap, at this spot, produced this many.*</p>\n<h3>Seasons — the reminders</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Seasons</strong> tab lists your state's furbearers A to Z. Tap one for its season dates, bag limits, and any notes. A badge shows whether it's open, closed, or coming up.</p>\n<p>Read the red line at the top of the tab: this is a **reminder, not legal authority**. Seasons change, emergency orders happen, counties differ. Check your state agency's current regulations before you trap — the app will never tell you it's legal to set a trap.</p>\n<h3>Licenses</h3>\n<p>Open the <strong>Licenses</strong> tab and tap \"📷 Add license photo.\" Snap your license (or your kid's, your buddy's — you can label whose is whose). It lives on your phone so it's there when the warden asks, even with no signal.</p>\n<h3>Backing up — read this one twice</h3>\n<p>There is no account and no cloud. Everything lives on your phone, which means a lost phone, a wiped phone, or clearing your browser data can take your whole season with it.</p>\n<ul><li>In <strong>Settings</strong>, tap <strong>\"⬇ Export catches (CSV)\"</strong> and</li></ul>\n<p><strong>\"⬇ Export sets (CSV)\"</strong> now and then. It downloads spreadsheet files you can keep, email to yourself, or open in Excel.</p>\n<ul><li>Do it at least a few times a season. If the phone goes in the creek, the</li></ul>\n<p>CSVs are your season.</p>\n<h3>If something looks wrong</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>The app looks old or stuck after an update:</strong> force-quit your browser</li></ul>\n<p>completely (swipe it away) and reopen the app from the home-screen icon. Nine times out of ten, that fixes it.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Your location dot won't show:</strong> check that you allowed location access</li></ul>\n<p>for the browser, and that you're not in airplane mode.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Something's genuinely broken or confusing:</strong> tell Tanner. That's what the</li></ul>\n<p>beta is for — he'd rather hear about it than have you fight it.</p>\n<h3>The beta deal</h3>\n<p>This is a closed beta — a small circle on purpose. Please don't pass the link around. If something looks wrong, or you want something the app doesn't do yet, tell Tanner directly.</p>\n<h3>The fine print</h3>\n<p>Season data is a reminder reference only — not legal authority. Know your regulations, check your local ordinances, and trap legal.</p>";
